Why you used ArrayList instead of array?
Arrays have a fixed size, so we would need to predict the maximum number of records, which is unrealistic.
ArrayList automatically resizes at runtime, making it suitable for add/remove operations.
It provides useful methods like add(), remove(), and iteration support, which simplifies service-layer logic.
Since this is an in-memory simulation of a real system, ArrayList closely represents how data grows in real applications (later replaced by DB tables).


Where did you use static members and why?
I used static members in the IdGenerator / service classes for generating unique IDs.
Static fields are shared across all objects, ensuring ID uniqueness.
IDs should not reset when a new service object is created.
It represents global application state, similar to auto-increment IDs in a database.


Where did you use inheritance and what did you gain from it?
I used inheritance by creating a Person base class and extending it with Student.
Code reuse: Common fields (id, name, email) are defined once.
Cleaner design: Avoids duplication across entities.
Polymorphism: Method overriding allows Student to customize behavior (getDisplayName()).
Scalability: Future entities like Instructor or Admin can extend Person easily.